<ArticleLayout slug="transcreation-as-a-production-line" ctaLabel="Adapt a campaign" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Transcreation goes wrong at the handoffs: new words laid over the same visual, or a brief that never said what was allowed to change. Run it as a line and each handoff gets an owner.</p>

  <h2>What is the transcreation process for marketing?</h2>

  <p>It is a line of work run from a signed-off brief, not a per-word order. The source campaign is audited for the target market, copy options come with back translations, the visual is adapted to match, and the set is reviewed and checked against local rules. hubStudio writes copy in-region and adapts the visual in the same line.</p>

  <p>The professional definition starts in the same place.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Institute of Translation and Interpreting defines transcreation of advertising and marketing content as a professional practice that combines translation, copywriting and intercultural consultancy, and that usually, but not always, involves adaptation.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Guidelines drafted from a member survey run October 2024 to April 2025 and focus groups with academics, transcreators and transcreation companies from January to September 2025.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">As a process, the same code describes transcreation as taking an agreed brief, including the source copy and other brand and company documents, and producing at least one option built to maximize appeal to and impact on the target audience. It classes transcreation as a translation-related activity, not a type of translation.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Association guidelines, no data behind the definition.</p>

  <p>The code covers advertising and marketing content only. It leaves out literary texts, non-promotional audiovisual work and video game localization, and it says plainly that it is not training material.</p>

  <h2>How is transcreation different from translation and localization?</h2>

  <p>Translation answers to the source. Transcreation answers to the audience. The instruments behind each term are different kinds of document, and the table says which.</p>

  <p>No standards-body or association definition of localization could be read for this page. China's translators' association publishes a localization terminology standard, but its definitions sit behind a member login. The localization row is this page's own working definition, and it is labeled that way.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Term</th>
          <th scope="col">Defined by</th>
          <th scope="col">What it covers</th>
          <th scope="col">What kind of source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Translation</th>
          <td>ISO 17100:2015; Institute of Translation and Interpreting</td>
          <td>The processes and resources a quality translation service needs; a result that conveys the meaning and nuances of the original</td>
          <td>International standard for the service, association guidance for the result</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Post-editing</th>
          <td>ISO 18587:2017</td>
          <td>Full, human post-editing of machine translation output, and the competences of post-editors</td>
          <td>International standard, adopted identically in China</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Transcreation</th>
          <td>Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026</td>
          <td>Translation, copywriting and intercultural consultancy combined, for advertising and marketing</td>
          <td>Association guidelines</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Localization</th>
          <td>This page's working definition</td>
          <td>Adapting a campaign's language, formats and conventions for one market</td>
          <td>No published definition could be read</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A good translation "accurately conveys the meaning and nuances of the original text," according to the Institute of Translation and Interpreting, which also notes that the translation and interpreting industry is not regulated.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting guidance article, published September 2023, updated August 2026. Association guidance, no survey behind it.</p>

  <p>That last clause matters for a buyer. With no regulator, the standards and the codes are the only shared yardstick.</p>

  <h2>Which standards apply to a transcreation line?</h2>

  <p>Three international standards touch the work, and none of the ones read for this page names transcreation in its scope.</p>

  <p>ISO 17100:2015, published in April 2015 and amended in 2017, is the translation services standard. It sets requirements for the core processes, resources and other aspects needed to deliver a quality translation service. Post-editing of machine translation sits in a separate standard, ISO 18587:2017, published in April 2017, which covers full, human post-editing and the competences of post-editors. Scope and dates for both were read on a standards catalog record in September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China adopted both standards nationally. GB/T 19363.1-2022 adopts ISO 17100:2015 and replaces a 2008 edition first issued in 2003, and GB/T 40036-2021 adopts ISO 18587:2017 as an identical standard.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the forewords of GB/T 19363.1-2022 and GB/T 40036-2021, as reproduced by the Translators Association of China, read September 2026. Standard forewords only; implementation dates not read.</p>

  <p>The review stage has its own reference. ISO 5060:2024, published in February 2024, gives guidance on evaluating human translation, post-edited machine translation and unedited machine translation output, using error categories and penalty scoring. It was read on one catalog record.</p>

  <p>The people doing the work have a published baseline too.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Translators Association of China lists the basic competences of translators and interpreters as foreign language ability, translation or interpreting ability, language processing, domain knowledge, research, information retrieval, cultural ability and technical ability, with each assessed on documentary evidence.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Translators Association of China group standard T/TAC 2-2017, basic competence requirements for interpreters and translators, foreword read September 2026.</p>

  <p>For transcreation itself, the instrument is the Institute of Translation and Interpreting's code. It calls itself guidelines, and says that for companies it serves as a set of industry standards. It adds attributes a translator's list does not carry: copywriting skills, awareness of both markets, brand and marketing knowledge, and the ability to explain choices through back translations and rationales.</p>

  <p>A buyer researching the subject rarely meets any of this on the pages that rank.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Across four phrasings a buyer would type, 28 of 31 results were published by sellers of translation or localization software or services. One came from a professional association, and none from a standards body.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search result review conducted 10 September 2026, four queries, every result classified by publisher type.</p>

  <h2>What are the stages of a transcreation production line?</h2>

  <p>The code sets out the association's model.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Institute of Translation and Interpreting's code sets out a six-step transcreation workflow: agree a brief, gather and analyze information, produce the options, collect client feedback and amend, finalize and sign off, and keep a record of the work where the contract allows.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Workflow drawn from practitioner handbooks and contributor suggestions; the code notes that real practice varies.</p>

  <p>The code treats transcreation as copy work. A campaign also has images, layouts and rules. The table below is this page's production view, mapped to the code where the code covers a stage. Visual adaptation and the compliance check are production practice, not steps in the code, and the whole table describes practice, not a measurement.</p>

