          <td>Production practice</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The code's last two steps close the line after the check: final sign-off by the client, then a record of the work where the agreement and any NDA allow it.</p>

  <p>The roles come from the code, not from an ISO standard. They are the client, the client's in-region representative, the transcreator and, where an agency is involved, its internal quality assurance.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The code has the transcreator supply a back translation and a rationale for every option submitted, recommends always offering at least two options, one bolder and one safer, and records two rounds of client feedback as common practice.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Association guidelines.</p>

  <p>The back translation is the handoff tool. It lets a reviewer who does not read the target language see what moved.</p>

  <h2>Where does meaning get lost between the copy and the visual?</h2>

  <p>At the join, and the code names the misunderstanding that causes it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Institute of Translation and Interpreting's code reports that most clients think transcreation means translating a few words while keeping the same visuals, and names visual and space constraints in the source copy as a standing challenge: an original visual that may not appeal to the local market, or a space that is not realistic for the target language.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Qualitative finding from member survey and focus group work; no share or count published.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Transcreation "involves varying degrees of adaptation or even a complete rewrite."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Association guidelines.</p>

  <p>A complete rewrite rarely fits a layout drawn for the original line. No method-stated measurement of how much meaning is lost at that join turned up in research for this page. The failure is documented as a pattern, not a rate.</p>

  <p>China's translators' association already treats the two as one object. Its quality assessment specification for localization judges the translation and the target-language typesetting together.</p>

  <p>The fix is organizational. List the visual in the brief as an input. Measure the space for each format before copy is written. Put the designer in front of the options before the client chooses. On marketplace work, hubStudio does both halves in one place.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"We write the Chinese copy in-region, with copywriters who know how Chinese shoppers read a detail page, and we adapt the visual system to match."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio ecommerce design service page, read 10 September 2026. First-party statement of practice, not a measured figure.</p>

  <h2>What does the compliance check catch?</h2>

  <p>Claims and labels that travel badly. This section describes production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <p>A superlative is the classic case. A line that says "best" in the source market can land on a banned term in China.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Advertising Law bans "national level," "highest grade," "best" and any term of the same or similar meaning in advertising, and sets a fine of 200,000 to 1,000,000 RMB on the advertiser for a breach, with the same band on the agency and the publisher.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Advertising Law of the People's Republic of China, Articles 9(3) and 57, 2021 amendment. Statutory text, no sample.</p>

  <p>If any image in the adapted set is generated, the labeling rules follow it into every copy that ships.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Measures for Labeling AI-Generated Synthetic Content, issued 14 March 2025 by four central bodies and effective 1 September 2025, require a user-perceivable label on generated images and video and a second, machine label written into the file metadata carrying the synthetic attribute, the provider identifier and a content number, and require that exported and downloaded copies still carry the visible label.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China announcement, 14 March 2025. The regulator's own published instrument, effective 1 September 2025.</p>

  <p>That is why the check sits after visual adaptation and before sign-off. It has to see the copy and the image together.</p>

  <h2>What goes in a transcreation brief?</h2>

  <p>Everything the line needs to decide before anyone writes.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"A clear and detailed brief is the point of departure. No transcreation can be started without one."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Association guidelines.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under that code the client supplies brand information, campaign objectives, the master copy including its visuals, a target audience profile, preferred tone and register, the final formats and any restrictions. The transcreator runs a cross-cultural audit of the campaign, and both sides agree the room for creativity, the number of options, the number of feedback rounds, the budget and the deadline, with the client's in-region representative in the room.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Association guidelines, no data.</p>

  <p>The template below is built from the code's first step and its example brief, and credited to the code. Rows marked production practice are this page's additions for a line that adapts the visual as well as the copy.</p>

