  <h2>How is transcreation priced?</h2>

  <p>By time or by scope, not by the word. That is the association's rule, and it is where buyers and practitioners most often talk past each other.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Institute of Translation and Interpreting's code says transcreation is priced like copywriting and usually above traditional translation. It is paid by the project as a flat fee, by the hour, sometimes on retainer, or by the day, and "transcreation is never paid per word."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Association guidelines, no rate figures published.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Where the work runs on a flat fee, the code has the parties fix the number of versions or a clear list of deliverables up front, including the back translation and rationale for each version and the number of feedback rounds. The same code reports clients still asking for per-word quotes.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. Association guidelines drawn from member survey and focus group work, no counts published.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"The cost of transcreation is the cost of the time it takes to come up with the final copy."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting guidance article, published November 2023, updated August 2026. Association guidance, no rate data.</p>

  <p>The units differ because they count different things.</p>

