  <p>The only band this page carries is for translation, and it comes without a method.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published rate cards in the translation category put professional English to Simplified Chinese at roughly $0.08 to $0.18 per source word, and price transcreation per hour or per project instead, because source word count does not track the creative work.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages collected across the category, September 2026, no company named. Rate cards only, no survey and no method published behind the band.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Translators Association of China's localization quotation specification, first released in October 2013, carries an appendix tying unit price to the translation memory match rate, the structure behind per-word pricing that discounts repeated text.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Translators Association of China, localization services quotation specification ZYF 001-2013, appendix titles read September 2026. Full text member-only.</p>

  <p>Match-rate discounts reward repetition. Transcreation is paid for the opposite.</p>

  <p>hubStudio's <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> lists product copy and transcreation under its asset-based buying model, and the same page says how the work is staffed.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"Transcreation is handled by people who write natively in the target language, not translated into it."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio pricing page, read 10 September 2026. First-party statement of practice, not a measured figure.</p>

  <p>What an adaptation costs once the re-shoot and re-layout are counted is covered in the <a href="/resources/insights/cost-to-localize-a-campaign-for-china">cost to localize a campaign for China article</a>. The <a href="/resources/insights/campaign-adaptation-cost-per-market">campaign adaptation cost per market article</a> sets out the ladder of adaptation jobs, from a copy swap to a rebuild.</p>

  <h2>Can AI be used in transcreation?</h2>

  <p>The two published views point different ways, and they answer different questions.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">GenAI use in translation reached 34 percent of respondents among 50 global brands, with another 23 percent planning to adopt it.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025. Survey of senior marketing procurement respondents at 50 global brands across more than ten sectors.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Institute of Translation and Interpreting's code takes the position that generative AI is not useful for transcreation work, citing missing human skills, confidentiality, unreliability, intellectual property risk and a lack of originality, and says it has already taken a share of the transcreation market.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Institute of Translation and Interpreting, Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026. A position statement from an association of practitioners; no usage or quality data published behind it.</p>

  <p>Neither settles the matter. The survey measures adoption in translation, not the quality of transcreated output. The code is a position, written by an association whose members do the work, with no usage or quality data behind it.</p>

  <p>Where machine output is edited by people, ISO 18587:2017 sets the requirements for full, human post-editing. And the brief can settle the practical questions: whether AI drafting is allowed on the job, and how client data is handled. The code asks agencies running their own AI platform to brief clients on data protection, non-disclosure and non-retention, so client material is not used to train it.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/solutions/ai-production/content">AI content production page</a> describes the production work these briefs feed, and the <a href="/solutions/brands">brands solutions page</a> covers multi-market transcreation for brand teams.</p>

  <h2>Questions about the transcreation process</h2>

  <h3>What is the difference between translation and transcreation?</h3>

  <p>Translation carries the meaning and nuances of the original text, and ISO 17100:2015 sets requirements for the service. Transcreation, as the Institute of Translation and Interpreting's August 2026 code defines it for advertising and marketing, combines translation, copywriting and intercultural consultancy, and usually involves adaptation. The code classes it as a translation-related activity, not a type of translation.</p>

  <h3>How does a transcreation process work?</h3>

  <p>It starts from a signed-off brief. The Institute's code sets six steps: agree the brief, gather and analyze information, produce options, collect feedback and amend, finalize and sign off, and keep a record. A production line adds visual adaptation and a compliance check, so the copy and the image leave together. Two feedback rounds are common practice.</p>

  <h3>Is there a standard for transcreation?</h3>

  <p>None of the standards read for this page names transcreation in its scope. ISO 17100:2015 covers translation services, ISO 18587:2017 covers human post-editing of machine translation, and ISO 5060:2024 covers evaluating translation output. The Institute of Translation and Interpreting's Transcreation Code of Practice, August 2026, addresses it directly, as association guidelines.</p>

  <h3>How is transcreation priced?</h3>

  <p>By the project, the hour or the day, according to the Institute of Translation and Interpreting's code, which says transcreation is never paid per word and is usually priced above traditional translation. Published translation rate cards collected in September 2026 put professional English to Simplified Chinese at roughly $0.08 to $0.18 per source word, with no method behind the band.</p>

  <h3>Can AI do transcreation?</h3>

  <p>The published views point different ways. The Institute of Translation and Interpreting's code takes the position that generative AI is not useful for transcreation work. A global advertiser trade body survey from March 2025 found 34 percent of respondents at 50 brands using it in translation, with 23 percent more planning to. Settle AI use and data handling in the brief.</p>
